 =20
    <meta name=3D"description" content=3D"Exhibition. Sep&nbsp;27, 2026=E2=
=80=93Jun&nbsp;13, 2027. =E2=80=9CThe artist=E2=80=99s role is to bear witn=
ess=E2=80=94to both the past and the present,=E2=80=9D says Nilima Sheikh. =
Over the course of her five-decade career, Sheikh has transformed narrative=
 painting as a new way of engaging with history, highlighting lived experie=
nces, illuminating regional stories, and foregrounding women=E2=80=99s voic=
es. Her research-based art practice draws on a vast array of historical acc=
ounts, archival materials, and literary sources. Adapting the traditional s=
croll format and painting primarily in casein and gum temperas, Sheikh weav=
es together image, text, and stenciled patterning to create multilayered na=
rratives that unfold as interconnected vignettes. Since the early 2000s, Sh=
eikh has often turned her attention to the Kashmir Valley, the culturally r=
ich and politically complex northernmost region of the Indian subcontinent.=
 Nilima Sheikh: Fractured Skies brings together selections from her series =
Each Night Put Kashmir in Your Dreams (2003=E2=80=9314) and Home, Land (202=
3=E2=80=9325) alongside Shahid/Witness (2025=E2=80=9326), a new, site-respo=
nsive set of paintings commissioned by the Kiran Nadar Museum of Art, New D=
elhi. These three major bodies of work trace the depth of Sheikh=E2=80=99s =
engagement with the region over the past two decades and reflect the broade=
r themes that inform her expansive practice: violence, displacement, loss, =
and the enduring power of human connection. Born in New Delhi, Sheikh was a=
 member of the generation of artists working and studying in Baroda (now Va=
dodara) in the late 1960s, who helped shape contemporary art in post-Partit=
ion India. Through her visionary painting practice, Sheikh has established =
herself as one of the most consequential artists working in South Asia toda=
y.">
