eikh. Over the course of her five-decade career, Sheikh has transformed nar=
rative painting as a new way of engaging with history, highlighting lived e=
xperiences, illuminating regional stories, and foregrounding women=E2=80=99=
s voices. Her research-based art practice draws on a vast array of historic=
al accounts, archival materials, and literary sources. Adapting the traditi=
onal scroll format and painting primarily in casein and gum temperas, Sheik=
h weaves together image, text, and stenciled patterning to create multilaye=
red narratives that unfold as interconnected vignettes. Since the early 200=
0s, Sheikh has often turned her attention to the Kashmir Valley, the cultur=
ally rich and politically complex northernmost region of the Indian subcont=
inent. Nilima Sheikh: Fractured Skies brings together selections from her s=
eries Each Night Put Kashmir in Your Dreams (2003=E2=80=9314) and Home, Lan=
d (2023=E2=80=9325) alongside Shahid/Witness (2025=E2=80=9326), a new, site=
-responsive set of paintings commissioned by the Kiran Nadar Museum of Art,=
 New Delhi. These three major bodies of work trace the depth of Sheikh=E2=
=80=99s engagement with the region over the past two decades and reflect th=
e broader themes that inform her expansive practice: violence, displacement=
, loss, and the enduring power of human connection. Born in New Delhi, Shei=
kh was a member of the generation of artists working and studying in Baroda=
 (now Vadodara) in the late 1960s, who helped shape contemporary art in pos=
t-Partition India. Through her visionary painting practice, Sheikh has esta=
blished herself as one of the most consequential artists working in South A=
sia today.">
