nk=3D"B">
   =20
  <p>=E2=80=9CThe artist=E2=80=99s role is to bear witness=E2=80=94to both =
the past and the present,=E2=80=9D says Nilima Sheikh. Over the course of h=
er five-decade career, Sheikh has transformed narrative painting as a new w=
ay of engaging with history, highlighting lived experiences, illuminating r=
egional stories, and foregrounding women=E2=80=99s voices. Her research-bas=
ed art practice draws on a vast array of historical accounts, archival mate=
rials, and literary sources. Adapting the traditional scroll format and pai=
nting primarily in casein and gum temperas, Sheikh weaves together image, t=
ext, and stenciled patterning to create multilayered narratives that unfold=
 as interconnected vignettes.</p>

<p>Since the early 2000s, Sheikh has often turned her attention to the Kash=
mir Valley, the culturally rich and politically complex northernmost region=
 of the Indian subcontinent. <em>Nilima Sheikh: Fractured Skies</em> brings=
 together selections from her series <em>Each Night Put Kashmir in Your Dre=
ams</em> (2003=E2=80=9314) and <em>Home, Land</em> (2023=E2=80=9325) alongs=
ide <em>Shahid/Witness</em> (2025=E2=80=9326), a new, site-responsive set o=
f paintings commissioned by the Kiran Nadar Museum of Art, New Delhi. These=
 three major bodies of work trace the depth of Sheikh=E2=80=99s engagement =
with the region over the past two decades and reflect the broader themes th=
at inform her expansive practice: violence, displacement, loss, and the end=
uring power of human connection.</p>

<p>Born in New Delhi, Sheikh was a member of the generation of artists work=
ing and studying in Baroda (now Vadodara) in the late 1960s, who helped sha=
pe contemporary art in post-Partition India. Through her visionary painting=
 practice, Sheikh has established herself as one of the most consequential =
artists working in South Asia today.</p><blockquote id=3D"credits">
      <p>Organized by The Museum of Modern Art, New York (MoMA), and the Ki=
ran Nadar Museum of Art, New Delhi (KNMA). The exhibition is organized by C=
ara Manes, Associate Curator, Department of Painting and Sculpture, MoMA, a=
nd Roobina Karode, Artistic Director and Chief Curator, KNMA, with Elizabet=
h Wickham, Curatorial Assistant, Department of Painting and Sculpture, MoMA=
, and Avijna Bhattacharya, Senior Curator, KNMA.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </div>


